Log — Day 2

1. Actual duration: ~[fill in] — pointer arithmetic + 2D matrix + reflection on cache.

2. New tools/commands: %p in printf, %zu for size_t, (void*) cast on pointers for address printing.

3. Bug + root cause: compilation error "array type has incomplete element type 'double[]'" when trying to remove both dimensions from the function signature — cause: the compiler needs the size of every dimension except the first to compute the offset between rows in pointer arithmetic.

4. Hypothesis tested and corrected: I initially thought the double[4][8] matrix was stored as "separate blocks"; I tested by printing the addresses of matrix[0][7] and matrix[1][0], and the difference of exactly 8 bytes confirmed it's a single contiguous block.

5. Confidence at checkpoints: grew throughout the day — I started "guessing" (contiguity, access order) and ended with my own empirical evidence, though I overestimated the performance gain ("cut in half" without a real numeric basis).

6. Open questions: none pending.

7. Connection to the project: double[4][8] matrix mentally mapped as OFDM symbols × subcarriers; contiguity and row-wise access connect directly to the thesis's efficiency/energy angle (cache locality reduces memory operations, relevant to computational/energy cost).

8. Source of help: Copilot for the code, less than the previous day; own research needed to figure out how to pass a matrix to a function and derive dimensions via sizeof.
